= Computer security =

== Overview ==
'''Computer security''' is the protection of computational systems, communications, services, and information against unauthorized access, disclosure, modification, destruction, disruption, or other harmful failure.

Security is defined relative to assets, threats, assumptions, and desired properties.

== Security objectives ==
Common objectives include:

* confidentiality;
* integrity;
* availability;
* authentication;
* authorization;
* accountability;
* non-repudiation where relevant;
* resilience and recovery.

No single list captures every security goal.

== Threat model ==
A threat model states what attackers can do, what assets matter, what trust assumptions exist, and which failures are in scope.

A mechanism can be secure under one threat model and inadequate under another.

== Authentication and authorization ==
'''Authentication''' establishes or verifies an identity or credential.

'''Authorization''' determines what an authenticated or otherwise identified principal may do.

Therefore:

<pre>
authentication ≠ authorization
</pre>

== Defense in depth ==
Security typically uses multiple layers:

* secure design;
* access controls;
* isolation;
* patching;
* monitoring;
* backups;
* cryptographic mechanisms;
* operational procedures;
* human processes.

A failure in one layer should not automatically compromise the whole system.

== Cryptography ==
[[Cryptography]] can protect confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, and other properties.

But secure cryptography does not guarantee a secure system.

Key management, implementation bugs, endpoint compromise, permissions, social engineering, and operational failures can bypass cryptographic protections.

== Security is not absolute ==
Security claims require scope and assumptions.

A system can reduce risk without being secure against every attacker or failure.

